1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,852
If It’s Complicated, Just Don’t

2
00:00:03,852 --> 00:00:35,720
The same day President Trump claimed Iran’s leaders were “begging” for a peace deal. Iran denied negotiating. The Revolutionary Guards announced a program called “For Iran,” allowing recruitment for security duties from the age of twelve. On March 11 an eleven-year-old boy had been killed at a Tehran checkpoint. Lebanon’s Health Ministry said the death toll since Israel’s invasion had risen to 1,116, and two Golani Brigade soldiers were killed.

3
00:00:36,320 --> 00:01:07,540
Nariman brought the atlas she had saved from the house that fell, its cover swollen with water. “Here. Abu Dhabi. They said two people died here today. Who shot that missile?” Professor Azad Moradi stopped for a moment. “Iran shot it.” “We did? Why did we?” To a seven-year-old the country was we. For seventy-two years he had used that word carefully. He knew too well who this country’s “we” put in and who it left out.

4
00:01:08,140 --> 00:01:38,328
“Because there are American soldiers there.” “But the people who died weren’t American soldiers.” “In a war the reasons are complicated, Nariman.” “If it’s complicated, then just don’t do it.” Azad could not answer. Evening light came in through the ventilation window. Spring was coming, even in a war. That was the logic of nature, simpler than the logic of people, and older. A seven-year-old’s logic beat the grown-ups’ war.

5
00:01:38,928 --> 00:02:08,060
Three in the morning. Only Captain Farhan Moradi was on duty. “I can’t sleep. Let’s have some tea.” The console still held one line from joint anti-smuggling work before the war, running through a relay station in Quetta to Islamabad. For twenty-eight days no one had used it. No one had cut it, either. “Did you see the order that came down today? Twelve.” Farhan’s laugh stopped. He answered by not answering.

6
00:02:08,660 --> 00:02:37,576
“My uncle used to say what’s frightening about soldiers is that they hand their thinking over to someone else. These days I’m not so sure.” Betrayal and loyalty begin with the same sentence: I am trying to protect this country. It is the next sentence where they part. In Seoul, Kang Yujin told her lab director Park Sejun about the model reacting to a variable she had not put in. “Who have you told?” His nod went on a little long.

7
00:02:38,176 --> 00:02:41,988
Epic Fury continues in Episode 11.
